### What is the Edit Distance problem?

Edit Distance (Levenshtein distance) asks for the minimum number of single-character operations needed to transform one string into another. The allowed operations are: insert a character, delete a character, and substitute a character. For example, transforming `"KITTEN"` into `"SITTING"` requires 3 edits.

```java
package com.bank.core;

// "KITTEN" -> "SITTING"
// Step 1: substitute K -> S: "SITTEN"
// Step 2: substitute E -> I: "SITTIN"
// Step 3: insert G at end: "SITTING"
// Edit distance = 3
```

### How does the 2D DP table work?

Define `dp[i][j]` as the minimum edits to transform the first `i` characters of A into the first `j` characters of B. The base cases handle transforming to or from an empty string: `dp[i][0] = i` (delete all i characters) and `dp[0][j] = j` (insert all j characters). For the rest of the table, if the characters match, no edit is needed and the cell copies the diagonal. If they differ, you consider three operations and take the cheapest.

```java
package com.bank.service;

public class EditDistance {

    public int minDistance(String a, String b) {
        int m = a.length();
        int n = b.length();
        int[][] dp = new int[m + 1][n + 1];

        for (int i = 0; i <= m; i++) dp[i][0] = i;
        for (int j = 0; j <= n; j++) dp[0][j] = j;

        for (int i = 1; i <= m; i++) {
            for (int j = 1; j <= n; j++) {
                if (a.charAt(i - 1) == b.charAt(j - 1)) {
                    dp[i][j] = dp[i - 1][j - 1]; // no edit
                } else {
                    dp[i][j] = 1 + Math.min(
                        dp[i - 1][j - 1], // substitute
                        Math.min(
                            dp[i - 1][j],  // delete from A
                            dp[i][j - 1]   // insert into A
                        )
                    );
                }
            }
        }
        return dp[m][n];
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        EditDistance solver = new EditDistance();
        System.out.println(solver.minDistance("KITTEN", "SITTING"));
        System.out.println(solver.minDistance("SALARY", "SLARY"));
    }
}
```

```text
3
1
```

### What do the three mismatch operations mean?

When characters differ, you have three choices. Substitute replaces A[i] with B[j], costing 1 edit plus whatever it took to match the prefixes before both characters (diagonal dp[i-1][j-1]). Delete removes A[i] from A, costing 1 plus the cost of matching A[1..i-1] with B[1..j] (top dp[i-1][j]). Insert adds B[j] to A, costing 1 plus the cost of matching A[1..i] with B[1..j-1] (left dp[i][j-1]).

```java
package com.bank.core;

// On mismatch at dp[i][j]:
// Substitute: dp[i-1][j-1] + 1  (replace A[i] with B[j], move diagonal)
// Delete:     dp[i-1][j] + 1    (remove A[i], move up)
// Insert:     dp[i][j-1] + 1    (add B[j] to A, move left)
// Take the minimum of all three
```

### How do you optimize space with rolling rows?

Since each row depends only on the previous row and the current row's earlier cells, you can use two 1D arrays. This reduces space from O(m * n) to O(min(m, n)) by choosing the shorter string as the column dimension.

```java
package com.bank.service;

public class EditDistanceOptimized {

    public int minDistance(String a, String b) {
        if (a.length() < b.length()) {
            String temp = a; a = b; b = temp;
        }
        int m = a.length();
        int n = b.length();
        int[] prev = new int[n + 1];
        int[] curr = new int[n + 1];

        for (int j = 0; j <= n; j++) prev[j] = j;

        for (int i = 1; i <= m; i++) {
            curr[0] = i;
            for (int j = 1; j <= n; j++) {
                if (a.charAt(i - 1) == b.charAt(j - 1)) {
                    curr[j] = prev[j - 1];
                } else {
                    curr[j] = 1 + Math.min(prev[j - 1],
                        Math.min(prev[j], curr[j - 1]));
                }
            }
            int[] temp = prev;
            prev = curr;
            curr = temp;
        }
        return prev[n];
    }
}
```

### How is Edit Distance related to LCS?

When only insertions and deletions are allowed (no substitutions), the minimum edit distance equals `m + n - 2 * LCS`. Adding substitution as a third operation makes edit distance strictly smaller or equal: a substitution replaces what would otherwise be a deletion followed by an insertion.

| Problem | Operations | Formula |
|---|---|---|
| Edit distance (Levenshtein) | Insert, delete, substitute | Own recurrence |
| Insert/delete-only distance | Insert, delete | m + n - 2 * LCS |
| LCS | N/A | dp[m][n] from LCS table |
| SCS | N/A | m + n - LCS |

```java
package com.bank.core;

// With substitution: edit("ABC", "AEC") = 1 (substitute B->E)
// Without substitution: edit("ABC", "AEC") = 2 (delete B, insert E)
// Substitution saves one step when both strings differ at one position
```

### What are the edge cases?

Edge cases include: one or both strings empty, both strings identical (distance 0), strings of different lengths, and strings with no common characters.

```java
package com.bank.core;

// Edge case 1: A="", B="ABC" -> 3 (insert A, B, C)
// Edge case 2: A="ABC", B="" -> 3 (delete A, B, C)
// Edge case 3: A=B="SAME" -> 0 (no edits needed)
// Edge case 4: A="AB", B="CD" -> 2 (substitute each)
// Edge case 5: A="A", B="A" -> 0
```

### What about a banking use case?

At the National Bank of India, the transfer system compares a typed beneficiary name against the registered name. A small edit distance suggests a harmless typo that can proceed with a warning, while a large distance blocks the transfer as a potential wrong beneficiary.

```java
package com.bank.service;

public class BeneficiaryValidator {

    private final EditDistance editDistance = new EditDistance();

    public String validateBeneficiary(String typed, String registered) {
        int distance = editDistance.minDistance(
            typed.toUpperCase(), registered.toUpperCase());
        if (distance == 0) {
            return "EXACT MATCH";
        } else if (distance <= 2) {
            return "LIKELY TYPO (distance=" + distance + "), proceed with warning";
        } else {
            return "MISMATCH (distance=" + distance + "), block transfer";
        }
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        BeneficiaryValidator validator = new BeneficiaryValidator();
        System.out.println(validator.validateBeneficiary("RAJSH", "RAJESH"));
        System.out.println(validator.validateBeneficiary("PRIYA", "PRIYA"));
        System.out.println(validator.validateBeneficiary("KUMAR", "RAJESH"));
    }
}
```

```text
LIKELY TYPO (distance=1), proceed with warning
EXACT MATCH
MISMATCH (distance=5), block transfer
```

### What are common mistakes?

The most common mistake is forgetting to initialize the base cases. Without `dp[i][0] = i` and `dp[0][j] = j`, the border cells default to zero and the entire computation produces wrong results. Another mistake is using the wrong cell for substitution (using top or left instead of diagonal).

```java
package com.bank.core;

// WRONG: missing base cases
int[][] dp = new int[m + 1][n + 1]; // all zeros!
// dp[3][0] should be 3, not 0

// CORRECT: explicit base case initialization
for (int i = 0; i <= m; i++) dp[i][0] = i;
for (int j = 0; j <= n; j++) dp[0][j] = j;
```

### What should a senior engineer know?

A senior engineer should know that Edit Distance has no known sub-quadratic worst-case algorithm, and the SETH conjecture suggests O(n^2) may be a hard lower bound. For production fuzzy matching on large dictionaries, approximate algorithms or BK-trees are used to avoid computing edit distance against every entry. The rolling-row optimization is essential for long strings, and Hirschberg's algorithm combines linear space with the ability to reconstruct the edit sequence.

```java
package com.bank.core;

// Senior notes:
// 1. No known sub-quadratic worst-case algorithm
// 2. SETH conjecture: O(n^2) may be a hard lower bound
// 3. BK-trees: index words by edit distance for fast fuzzy search
// 4. Hirschberg: linear space + full reconstruction
// 5. Applications: spell check, DNA alignment, fuzzy matching
```
